  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The lead they held for four innings disappeared in one bad inning. That's the margin in the division.",
    "what_this_means": "Cam Schlittler was clean through five, nine strikeouts, nothing given away. The lineup got to Boston early, Caballero's home run staking them to a lead that looked safe. Then the fifth happened. Four runs came across before anyone got warm. That's where this game turned. Not in a rally or a late chance, but in the sudden collapse of an inning that should have been routine. The bullpen did what it could after: Blackburn, Hill, De los Santos, Yarbrough all clean, all quiet. It didn't matter. They'd spent what they had offensively on two runs and left three more runners on base in the ninth when the game was still within reach. Ten strikeouts and eight hits added up to nothing when it counted. They had the game. They let one inning take it.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is what fragile looks like from inside. A good starter, a competent pen, a lineup that scraped together some offense, and still it evaporated in four pitches. The inconsistency isn't theoretical anymore. It's the shape of every close game they're playing. They can't afford nights where one bad stretch erases the work that came before it. This division doesn't give that back.",
